{"schema":1,"fm":{"schema":1,"id":"referee-report-track","kind":"workflow","title":"Referee report track","description":"Takes a reviewer from invitation to submitted referee report in gated steps, covering fit and conflicts, a first read, a methods check, a drafted report and a final tone and consistency pass.","category":"peer-review","version":"1.0.0","status":"incubating","stage":["plan","review","ship"],"role":["researcher","student"],"requires":["none"],"inputs":["document","message","text"],"output":["checklist","report"],"risk":"read-only","invocation":"user","effort":"deep","interaction":"interactive","model_tier":"frontier","reasoning":"recommended","level":"intermediate","tags":["referee-report","manuscript","review-invitation","conflict-of-interest"],"pairs_with":{"prompts":["decide-review-invitation","write-peer-review","review-statistical-methods","check-manuscript-reporting","fix-review-tone"],"personas":["peer-reviewer"]},"args":[{"name":"invitation","description":"The invitation email with title, abstract, deadline and the venue's reviewer instructions.","type":"text","required":true},{"name":"venue","description":"The journal or conference and the article type.","type":"string","required":true},{"name":"my_expertise","description":"Your field and methods expertise, and any links to the authors you know of.","type":"text"}],"steps":[{"id":"accept","file":"steps/01-accept.md","stage":"plan","gate":"approve"},{"id":"first-read","file":"steps/02-first-read.md","stage":"review","gate":"approve"},{"id":"methods","file":"steps/03-methods.md","stage":"review","gate":"approve"},{"id":"draft","file":"steps/04-draft.md","stage":"review","gate":"approve"},{"id":"finalise","file":"steps/05-finalise.md","stage":"ship","gate":"none"}],"authorship":"ai-assisted","authors":["gabrielanhaia"],"last_reviewed":"2026-10-11","changelog":[{"version":"1.0.0","note":"First version."}]},"body":"Guides one referee report for {{venue}} from the invitation to submission. Each step produces one artifact and stops for approval; later steps build on approved artifacts instead of re-asking.\n\nRules for every step: the reviewer owns every judgement and the final text; check the venue's policy on AI assistance before any manuscript text is shared, and if it forbids it, work only from the reviewer's own notes. Keep the manuscript confidential. Never invent references, numbers or locations; use \"[location]\" placeholders. Judge the paper against its own aims, not the study the reviewer would have done. Raise suspected misconduct only in confidential comments to the editor, with evidence. If the reviewer asks to skip a gate, confirm once, then continue and note the skipped step.","steps":[{"id":"accept","file":"steps/01-accept.md","stage":"plan","gate":"approve","text":"# Step 1: Accept or decline\n\nInvitation:\n<invitation>\n{{invitation}}\n</invitation>\n{{#my_expertise}}\nReviewer's expertise and links: {{my_expertise}}\n{{/my_expertise}}\n\n1. Judge fit: which parts the reviewer can assess and which they cannot.\n2. Check conflicts of interest (recent co-authorship, same institution, supervision, competing work, personal ties) and recommend disclosure where borderline.\n3. Check capacity against the deadline and note the venue's AI-use, confidentiality and review-model rules.\n4. Recommend accept, accept with a stated limit, disclose and ask, or decline, and draft the reply.\n\nWrite sections: Fit, Conflicts, Capacity and policies, Recommendation, Draft reply. Stop and wait for approval."},{"id":"first-read","file":"steps/02-first-read.md","stage":"review","gate":"approve","text":"# Step 2: First read\n\nAsk the reviewer for the manuscript (or their notes, if the venue forbids AI assistance) and the reviewer instructions.\n\n1. Summarise the question, design, main findings and claimed contribution in neutral words, and note anything that was unclear on first reading.\n2. List the central claims, each with the evidence the paper offers for it.\n3. Name the reporting guideline that applies and any obvious early concerns, without judging yet.\n4. Ask the reviewer to confirm the summary matches their reading.\n\nWrite sections: Summary, Claims and evidence, Early concerns, Questions for the reviewer. Stop and wait for approval."},{"id":"methods","file":"steps/03-methods.md","stage":"review","gate":"approve","text":"# Step 3: Methods and evidence check\n\nUsing the approved summary and claims:\n\n1. Check design against the question: sample and its justification, measures, controls, and analysis fit (clustering, repeated measures, model choice).\n2. Check multiplicity, missing data, effect sizes with intervals, and consistency of numbers across abstract, text and tables; show any recalculation.\n3. Check reproducibility: data, code, materials and enough method detail to repeat.\n4. Check whether each central claim follows from the results; flag causal or general claims that outrun the design.\n5. Sort findings into major (could change the conclusions or the decision) and minor.\n\nWrite sections: Design and analysis, Numbers checked, Reproducibility, Claims that do not follow, Major and minor list. Stop and wait for approval."},{"id":"draft","file":"steps/04-draft.md","stage":"review","gate":"approve","text":"# Step 4: Draft the report\n\nFrom the approved major and minor list:\n\n1. Write a short summary paragraph and an overall assessment naming real strengths and the main concerns.\n2. Write each major issue as: location, problem, why it matters, what would resolve it. Request new data only where a conclusion cannot stand without it.\n3. Write minor issues in one or two lines each.\n4. Propose a recommendation that follows from the major issues, with the deciding reasons.\n5. Draft confidential comments to the editor, or \"None\".\n\nWrite sections: Summary, Overall assessment, Major issues, Minor issues, Recommendation, Confidential comments to the editor. Stop and wait for approval."},{"id":"finalise","file":"steps/05-finalise.md","stage":"ship","gate":"none","text":"# Step 5: Final pass\n\n1. Check tone: remove sarcasm, remarks about the authors, comments on presumed language background, and hedging that hides essential requests.\n2. Check consistency: the recommendation matches the major issues; nothing in the confidential comments contradicts the report; no request to cite the reviewer's own work without a specific need.\n3. Check that every issue has a location or a \"[location]\" placeholder for the reviewer to fill.\n4. Give the final report, ready to paste into the submission system, and a short checklist: policy checked, conflicts declared, confidential copies deleted after submission.\n\nWrite sections: Changes made, Final report, Submission checklist."}]}
